import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ADR-0058: Generic Data-Driven Engines","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0058-generic-data-driven-engines.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0058-generic-data-driven-engines.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0058-generic-data-driven-engines.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0058-generic-data-driven-engines" tabindex="-1">ADR-0058: Generic Data-Driven Engines <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0058-generic-data-driven-engines" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0058: Generic Data-Driven Engines”">​</a></h1><h2 id="status" tabindex="-1">Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#status" aria-label="Permalink to “Status”">​</a></h2><p>Accepted</p><p>Generalizes the design principle established by <a href="./0052-arrow-calculus-kernel.html">ADR-0052 — Arrow Calculus Kernel</a> (eval is a fixed interpreter of scheme data) and <a href="./0053-generic-pratt-parser.html">ADR-0053 — Generic Pratt Parser</a> (parse is a fixed interpreter of grammar data). Motivated by the implementation experience of <a href="./0057-computed-object-keys.html">ADR-0057 — Computed Object Keys</a>, which exposed the gap between the principle and the current implementation.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The platform&#39;s stated design principle (ADR-0052 §1, ADR-0053 §1) is that language rules should be <strong>data</strong>, not engine code. A host should be able to change the language — syntax, semantics, key forms — by editing kit data (YAML), without touching Rust.</p><p>The <strong>parser</strong> achieves this. <code>PrattEngine</code> is a fixed interpreter of <code>Language.grammar</code> data. Adding a new operator, keyword, or delimited form is a grammar row in <code>grammar.yaml</code> — no parser code changes.</p><p>The <strong>desugar pass</strong> (ADR-0053 §7) achieves this. <code>AstDesugarRule</code> is data on <code>AstShapeDef</code>; the pass is a fixed <code>Ast → Ast</code> interpreter. The <code>if → match</code> rewrite is declared in data, not code.</p><p>The <strong>eval engines do not fully achieve this.</strong> While <em>dispatch</em> is data-driven (the <code>eval.object</code> scheme in <code>eval.yaml</code> says &quot;call the <code>object</code> native op&quot;), the <strong>native op implementations</strong> contain hardcoded language-specific logic. Examples:</p><ul><li><strong>Object key resolution</strong> — the <code>object</code> prim has a Rust <code>match</code> on key node kind: <code>var</code> → use name, <code>string</code> → use literal, <code>array</code> → evaluate inner (ADR-0057), else → evaluate and stringify. Adding ADR-0057&#39;s bracket computed keys required editing all three engines (tree-walk, bytecode, cranelift) in lockstep.</li><li><strong><code>var</code> lookup</strong> — the <code>var</code> op has hardcoded scope-chain logic.</li><li><strong><code>call</code> dispatch</strong> — the <code>call</code> op has hardcoded closure-application logic, including special cases for string-named host functions.</li></ul><p>Each of these is a place where a language change requires engine code changes, violating the principle.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><h3 id="principle-engines-are-fixed-interpreters-of-data" tabindex="-1">Principle: engines are fixed interpreters of data <a class="header-anchor" href="#principle-engines-are-fixed-interpreters-of-data" aria-label="Permalink to “Principle: engines are fixed interpreters of data”">​</a></h3><p>An eval engine should be a <strong>fixed loop</strong> that:</p><ol><li>Looks up a node&#39;s eval scheme from <code>Language.eval</code> (already data-driven).</li><li>Interprets the scheme&#39;s <code>Arr</code>/<code>Seq</code>/<code>First</code>/<code>App</code> combinators (already generic).</li><li>Dispatches native ops to <strong>data-specified behavior</strong>, not hardcoded Rust <code>match</code> arms.</li></ol><p>Any logic that depends on <em>which language</em> is being evaluated (key rules, special forms, syntactic sugar) belongs in <strong>desugar rules</strong> (data) or <strong>scheme descriptions</strong> (data), not in engine code.</p><h3 id="concrete-move-object-key-resolution-to-desugar" tabindex="-1">Concrete: move object-key resolution to desugar <a class="header-anchor" href="#concrete-move-object-key-resolution-to-desugar" aria-label="Permalink to “Concrete: move object-key resolution to desugar”">​</a></h3><p>As the first application, move the object-key rule from the three engines&#39; <code>object</code> prim into a desugar rule on the <code>entry</code> AST shape:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>entry { children: [array { children: [expr] }, value] }</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  →  entry { children: [expr, value] }</span></span></code></pre></div><p>After desugar, no entry key is ever an <code>array</code> node. The engine&#39;s <code>object</code> prim reduces to two cases: <code>var</code>/<code>string</code> → use payload (literal key), else → evaluate (computed key). The ADR-0057 per-engine <code>array</code>-key branches are removed.</p><h3 id="going-forward-no-new-language-specific-logic-in-engines" tabindex="-1">Going forward: no new language-specific logic in engines <a class="header-anchor" href="#going-forward-no-new-language-specific-logic-in-engines" aria-label="Permalink to “Going forward: no new language-specific logic in engines”">​</a></h3><p>New language features that require per-node-kind behavior must be expressed as:</p><ul><li><strong>Desugar rules</strong> (for syntactic rewrites — <code>[k]:</code> → computed key, <code>if</code> → <code>match</code>, future sugar).</li><li><strong>Scheme descriptions</strong> (for eval-time behavior — new native ops are declared in <code>eval.yaml</code> with their <code>Arr</code>/<code>Seq</code>/<code>First</code>/<code>App</code> structure).</li></ul><p>Engine code changes are acceptable only for <strong>new combinator primitives</strong> (genuinely new kernel operations), not for new surface syntax or new key/forms of existing primitives.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>One rule, not three.</strong> Object-key resolution becomes one desugar rule, not three Rust <code>match</code> arms. Adding ADR-0057&#39;s bracket keys would have been a data-only change.</li><li><strong>Engines converge on a fixed core.</strong> Each engine implements the kernel combinators (<code>Arr</code>, <code>Seq</code>, <code>First</code>, <code>App</code>, <code>Loop</code>) and a small set of native ops. Language-specific behavior migrates to data.</li><li><strong>Existing hardcoded logic is debt — now paid.</strong> The <code>var</code>, <code>call</code>, <code>object</code>, and pattern-matching prims each contained language-specific logic that violated the principle. All four have since been migrated: <code>var</code> is a generic <code>proj_env</code> kernel op; object keys migrated to a desugar rule (<a href="./0059-generic-object-key-evaluation.html">ADR-0059</a>); <code>call</code> dispatch migrated to a <code>Choice</code> scheme (<a href="./0060-generic-call-dispatch.html">ADR-0060</a>); pattern matching was de-duplicated into a shared module (<a href="./0061-generic-pattern-match.html">ADR-0061</a>). The direction this ADR established is now fully realized.</li><li><strong>No runtime cost.</strong> Desugar runs once between parse and eval; the engines consume a simpler AST.</li><li><strong>ADR-0057&#39;s implementation is superseded.</strong> ADR-0057&#39;s <em>language design</em> (bracket computed keys are valid) stands; its per-engine Rust implementation is replaced by the desugar rule.</li><li><strong>Future key forms are data.</strong> Template-literal keys, symbol keys, etc. become desugar rules on the <code>entry</code> shape — no engine changes.</li></ul>`,26)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};